# Long-case expansion L2 (target ~30 Qs): systemic sclerosis, dermatomyositis, erythroderma, lichen planus, atopic dermatitis, contact dermatitis.
# Own words; viva-length answers. Facts from IADVL 5e and current guidelines.
D = [
dict(case="Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)", qa=[
 ("How do you examine a patient with suspected SSc (clinical case approach)?", ["Hands: puffy fingers, sclerodactyly, digital pits/ulcers/gangrene, calcinosis, flexion contractures, nailfold capillaries (hand lens/dermoscope), resorbed tips, tendon friction rubs", "Face: microstomia (measure inter-incisor distance), radial furrows, beaked nose, mat telangiectasia, salt-and-pepper pigmentation, expressionless face", "Skin score: modified Rodnan skin score (17 sites, 0–3); proximal extent decides limited vs diffuse", "Systemic: BP (renal crisis), chest (basal 'velcro' crackles — ILD; loud P2 — PAH), dysphagia, reflux, joint contractures, muscle weakness"]),
 ("Describe the modified Rodnan skin score.", ["Skin thickness by palpation (0 normal, 1 mild, 2 moderate, 3 severe — cannot pinch) at 17 sites: fingers, hands, forearms, upper arms, thighs, legs, feet (bilateral) + face, anterior chest, abdomen; maximum 51; used to classify and monitor dcSSc (peaks in first 3–5 years then softens)"]),
 ("What is nailfold capillaroscopy and its patterns?", ["Non-invasive examination of nailfold capillaries (videocapillaroscopy/ dermoscope with oil)", "Scleroderma pattern (Cutolo): early — few giant capillaries and haemorrhages, preserved density; active — many giant capillaries and haemorrhages, moderate loss; late — severe capillary loss, avascular areas, ramified/bushy capillaries", "Useful to differentiate primary vs secondary Raynaud's and predict progression to SSc"]),
 ("What is scleroderma renal crisis — features and treatment?", ["Abrupt onset of severe hypertension (sometimes normotensive), acute kidney injury, microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, headache, retinopathy, pulmonary oedema; usually within 4 years of dcSSc; risk — anti-RNA polymerase III, rapidly progressive skin, prednisolone >15 mg/day", "Treatment: ACE inhibitor (captopril titrated quickly) even if creatinine rising, add CCB/ARB for BP, dialysis if needed (renal recovery possible over 2 years); avoid nephrotoxins; monitor BP at home"]),
 ("Discuss digital ulcer management.", ["Prevention: stop smoking, keep warm, CCB, PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil/tadalafil), bosentan for recurrent ulcers (prevents new ulcers)", "Active ulcers: IV iloprost (prostacyclin analogue) 3–5 days, PDE5 inhibitors, analgesia, wound care (moist dressings), treat infection (osteomyelitis risk), digital sympathectomy/ botulinum toxin, fat grafting; amputation for gangrene"]),
 ("Explain the pathogenesis of oesophageal involvement.", ["Smooth-muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the lower two-thirds of oesophagus with neural damage → hypomotility, incompetent lower oesophageal sphincter → reflux, heartburn, dysphagia, strictures, Barrett's oesophagus, aspiration (worsens ILD); manometry shows absent peristalsis; treat with PPIs, prokinetics, head-end elevation, small meals"]),
 ("Interstitial lung disease in SSc — screening and treatment.", ["Commonest cause of SSc-related death; risk — dcSSc, anti-Scl-70, African descent; screen all at diagnosis with HRCT and PFT (FVC, DLCO), then PFT every 6–12 months", "HRCT: NSIP pattern (ground glass with reticulation, basal), UIP less often", "Treatment: mycophenolate (SLS-II), cyclophosphamide (SLS-I), tocilizumab (early progressive), rituximab, nintedanib (antifibrotic — slows FVC decline), lung transplant"]),
 ("What is pulmonary arterial hypertension in SSc?", ["Late complication of lcSSc (anti-centromere), progressive dyspnoea, loud P2, right heart failure; screening annually with echo, PFT (DLCO/FVC ratio), NT-proBNP (DETECT algorithm); right heart catheterisation (mPAP >20 mmHg, PVR >2 WU) confirms; treat with ERA + PDE5i combination, prostacyclins, riociguat; poor prognosis"]),
 ("What is eosinophilic fasciitis (Shulman syndrome)?", ["Scleroderma-like disorder: acute symmetrical painful swelling then induration of limbs (sparing hands/face and fingers), peau d'orange, groove sign (venous furrows on elevating limb), after strenuous exercise; peripheral eosinophilia, hypergammaglobulinaemia; no Raynaud's; full-thickness biopsy to fascia/ MRI shows fascial thickening", "Treatment: prednisolone ± methotrexate; associated haematological disorders (aplastic anaemia) occasionally"]),
 ("Name other scleroderma mimics and how to differentiate.", ["Scleredema (Buschke) — diabetes/post-streptococcal/ paraprotein; upper back and neck woody induration, no Raynaud's", "Scleromyxoedema — paraproteinaemia (IgG-λ), waxy papules, 'Shar-Pei' sign, doughnut sign on knuckles, systemic (dermato-neuro syndrome); IVIG", "Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis — gadolinium in renal failure, limbs, brawny induration", "Chronic sclerodermoid GVHD, porphyria cutanea tarda (sclerodermoid), POEMS, phenylketonuria, stiff skin syndrome, morphoea"]),
 ("What are the newer and future treatments in SSc?", ["Autologous haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for early rapidly progressive dcSSc with poor prognosis (SCOT, ASTIS trials — improved survival, treatment-related mortality risk)", "Rituximab, tocilizumab (anti-IL-6R), abatacept, JAK inhibitors, nintedanib, anti-fibrotic research (lenabasum failed); CAR-T cell therapy (CD19) in refractory SSc — early reports"]),
 ("Describe musculoskeletal involvement.", ["Arthralgia, synovitis, tendon friction rubs (leathery creak over tendons — dcSSc, poor prognosis), flexion contractures, acro-osteolysis (resorption of distal phalanges), calcinosis, myopathy/myositis (overlap with anti-PM/Scl, anti-Ku)"]),
 ("What is the prognosis by subtype?", ["dcSSc: 10-year survival ~50–70%; early (first 5 years) high risk of renal crisis, ILD, myocardial involvement", "lcSSc: slower course, 10-year survival ~70–90%; late PAH and GI complications"]),
 ("Raynaud's phenomenon — primary versus secondary (table).", ["Primary: young women, symmetrical, mild, no ulcers/pitting, normal nailfold capillaries, ANA negative, normal ESR", "Secondary: older onset (>30 y), asymmetric, severe, painful, digital ulcers/gangrene, abnormal capillaroscopy, ANA/SSc antibodies, raised ESR, features of CTD"]),
 ("What are the cutaneous signs of SSc on dermoscopy and imaging?", ["Dermoscopy/ capillaroscopy as above; high-frequency ultrasound for skin thickness; plain X-ray hands — calcinosis, acro-osteolysis; thermography for Raynaud's"]),
 ("Describe mixed / overlap syndromes involving SSc.", ["SSc–myositis overlap (anti-PM/Scl, anti-Ku), SSc–SLE, SSc–RA, SSc–Sjögren's, MCTD (anti-U1-RNP); management targets the dominant organ involvement"]),
 ("Counselling points for an SSc patient.", ["Chronic disease with organ-based screening (yearly echo/PFT), stop smoking, protect hands from cold (gloves, warm clothes), skin care with emollients, physiotherapy/stretching to prevent contractures, oral hygiene/dental care (microstomia), small meals for reflux, home BP monitoring in dcSSc, pregnancy planning when disease stable (avoid in early dcSSc)"]),
 ("What drugs/toxins cause scleroderma-like disease?", ["Bleomycin, taxanes (docetaxel), gemcitabine, pentazocine (injection-site fibrosis), vitamin K injections (Texier's disease), appetite suppressants, L-tryptophan (eosinophilia–myalgia syndrome), toxic oil syndrome (rapeseed oil), vinyl chloride, silica (Erasmus syndrome), organic solvents, epoxy resins, checkpoint inhibitors"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Dermatomyositis", qa=[
 ("Describe the less common skin signs of DM.", ["Mechanic's hands (antisynthetase), palmar papules and ulcers over joint creases (MDA5 — 'inverse Gottron's'), flagellate erythema (scratch-like linear erythema on back), 'V-neck', shawl, holster signs, scalp dermatomyositis (scaly itchy, mimics psoriasis/seb derm), poikiloderma, calcinosis, panniculitis, erythroderma, vesiculobullous DM (malignancy), ovoid palatal patch (TIF1γ), 'red on white' lesions, Wong-type DM (PRP-like follicular hyperkeratosis)"]),
 ("What is the mechanism of photosensitivity and itch in DM?", ["UV-induced keratinocyte apoptosis increases autoantigen exposure (Mi-2, TIF1γ) and type I IFN production; itch is prominent (IL-31, IFN) — helps differentiate from lupus; photoprotection is essential"]),
 ("Muscle biopsy findings.", ["Perifascicular atrophy (pathognomonic), perivascular and perimysial inflammation (CD4 T, B cells, plasmacytoid DCs), MxA overexpression in perifascicular fibres (type I IFN), capillary dropout with complement (MAC) deposition in capillaries", "Polymyositis — endomysial CD8 T-cell invasion; IMNM — necrotic fibres with little inflammation (anti-SRP, anti-HMGCR — statins)"]),
 ("What is the antisynthetase syndrome?", ["Anti-aminoacyl tRNA synthetase antibodies (Jo-1 commonest, PL-7, PL-12, EJ, OJ, KS): myositis + ILD + non-erosive arthritis + mechanic's hands + Raynaud's + fever; ILD determines prognosis — treat with steroids + MMF/tacrolimus/ rituximab"]),
 ("Clinically amyopathic DM — what is important?", ["Skin-predominant (≥6 months) with normal or subclinical muscle tests; still needs malignancy screening and ILD screening — especially anti-MDA5 (rapidly progressive ILD, high mortality in Asians: treat early with steroids + calcineurin inhibitor + cyclophosphamide/ JAK inhibitor; ferritin and KL-6 track severity)"]),
 ("Calcinosis in juvenile DM — types and treatment.", ["Superficial plaques/ nodules, deep tumoral masses, intermuscular fascial plane calcification, exoskeleton-like (severe); can ulcerate and extrude chalky material", "Prevention: early aggressive immunosuppression (MTX + steroids, IVIG); treatment: diltiazem, bisphosphonates, sodium thiosulphate (IV/intralesional/topical), probenecid, colchicine, surgery for symptomatic deposits, JAK inhibitors (case series)"]),
 ("How is DM disease activity measured?", ["CDASI (Cutaneous Dermatomyositis Disease Area and Severity Index — activity and damage), MMT-8 (manual muscle testing of 8 muscle groups), CK, HAQ, IMACS core set measures, total improvement score (ACR/EULAR 2016)"]),
 ("Physiotherapy and rehabilitation role.", ["Early supervised exercise is safe and improves strength and function (even in active disease); prevents contractures; swallowing therapy for dysphagia; dietary advice, aspiration precautions"]),
 ("Differential diagnosis of proximal weakness with rash.", ["SLE/MCTD overlap, hypothyroid myopathy, steroid myopathy, statin myopathy (anti-HMGCR), trichinosis, sarcoid myopathy, HIV myositis, muscular dystrophies (no rash), inclusion body myositis (distal/ quadriceps, finger flexors, older men, no rash)"]),
 ("Describe heliotrope rash and its differentials.", ["Violaceous erythema ± oedema of upper eyelids (sometimes lower), may extend to periorbital skin; differentials — allergic contact dermatitis (eyelids), angioedema, SLE, trichinosis, nephrotic oedema, rosacea, atopic eyelid dermatitis, myxoedema"]),
 ("What drugs can induce DM-like eruptions?", ["Hydroxyurea (DM-like on knuckles without myositis), statins, penicillamine, TNF inhibitors, checkpoint inhibitors, BCG vaccine, interferons, NSAIDs"]),
 ("Discuss IVIG in DM.", ["2 g/kg over 2–5 days monthly — proven (ProDERM trial) for skin and muscle; also refractory dysphagia, calcinosis prevention in JDM; side effects — headache, aseptic meningitis, thrombosis, renal failure (sucrose-containing), anaphylaxis in IgA deficiency; costly"]),
 ("How do you monitor for malignancy over time?", ["Screen at diagnosis and annually for 3 years (risk highest in first 3 years); repeat CT chest/abdomen/pelvis ± PET-CT in high-risk (TIF1γ, NXP2, older age, dysphagia, cutaneous necrosis); gender-specific (mammography, Pap/ CA-125 and pelvic ultrasound, PSA), colonoscopy, nasopharyngoscopy in Southeast Asians"]),
 ("What is the role of hydroxychloroquine in DM skin and its risk?", ["Useful for photosensitive skin lesions with sunscreen; but drug eruptions (morbilliform) occur much more often in DM than lupus (~30%, especially anti-SAE positive); quinacrine or methotrexate alternatives; monitor eyes"]),
 ("Prognostic factors in DM.", ["Poor: malignancy, ILD (MDA5, antisynthetase), cardiac involvement, dysphagia/ aspiration pneumonia, older age, male sex, delayed treatment, high CK in adults with severe weakness; juvenile DM has good survival but calcinosis morbidity"]),
 ("What are the overlap myositis antibodies?", ["Anti-PM/Scl (SSc–myositis overlap, mechanic's hands, calcinosis, ILD), anti-Ku (SLE/ SSc overlap), anti-U1RNP (MCTD), anti-Ro52 (with antisynthetase/ ILD — severe), anti-SRP and anti-HMGCR (necrotising myopathy)"]),
 ("Pregnancy and DM.", ["Disease activity at conception predicts outcome; risks of fetal loss and prematurity with active disease; prednisolone, azathioprine, IVIG, HCQ, tacrolimus can be used; stop methotrexate/ MMF/ cyclophosphamide before conception"]),
 ("Why is anti-TIF1γ important in children vs adults?", ["In juvenile DM it is the commonest antibody and is NOT linked to cancer (associated with severe skin disease, lipodystrophy and chronic course); in adults it strongly predicts malignancy (≈ 20–65%)"]),
 ("Treatment of refractory skin disease in DM.", ["Methotrexate, MMF, IVIG, dapsone, JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, ruxolitinib — strong efficacy on skin and MDA5-ILD), apremilast, lenalidomide/ thalidomide, rituximab (better for muscle), anifrolumab under trial; antihistamines/ gabapentin for itch"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Erythroderma", qa=[
 ("How do you approach an erythroderma patient in the ward (step-by-step)?", ["Stabilise: vitals, temperature, hydration, urine output, sepsis screen, cardiac status", "History: prior dermatosis, drugs (last 8 weeks), atopy, photosensitivity, systemic symptoms (weight loss, nodes, itch severity), family history", "Examine for clues: scalp, nails, palms/soles, islands of sparing, mucosa, lymph nodes, hepatosplenomegaly", "Investigate: bloods, multiple biopsies, Sézary screen, KOH/scrapings, IgE, imaging", "Treat: supportive + cause-specific; avoid starting many new drugs"]),
 ("Drug-induced erythroderma — which drugs and features?", ["Carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbitone, allopurinol, dapsone, sulfonamides, β-lactams, vancomycin, anti-TB drugs, gold, lithium, calcium channel blockers, NSAIDs; often part of DRESS (fever, facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia, hepatitis) — RegiSCAR scoring; onset 2–8 weeks after drug; fastest resolution after withdrawal"]),
 ("Describe erythrodermic psoriasis triggers and management.", ["Triggers: abrupt withdrawal of systemic steroids/ potent topicals, phototherapy burn, infection, HIV, stress, lithium/antimalarials, alcohol", "Management: admit; bland emollients; cyclosporine 3–5 mg/kg (rapid) or infliximab/ IL-17/IL-23 inhibitors; methotrexate or acitretin for maintenance; avoid steroids; fluids and temperature control"]),
 ("What is the 'red man syndrome' (idiopathic erythroderma of elderly)?", ["Chronic idiopathic erythroderma in elderly men with marked itch, lichenification, palmoplantar keratoderma, dermatopathic lymphadenopathy, raised IgE; evolves to CTCL in some — repeated biopsies and T-cell clonality/flow cytometry every 6–12 months"]),
 ("Neonatal and infantile erythroderma — causes and approach.", ["Ichthyoses (collodion baby, congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma, Netherton — bamboo hair, hypernatraemic dehydration), SSSS, seborrhoeic/atopic dermatitis, immunodeficiency (Omenn, SCID, Wiskott–Aldrich, hyper-IgE), diffuse cutaneous mastocytosis, metabolic (biotinidase, multiple carboxylase, zinc, essential fatty acid deficiency), maternal GVHD, psoriasis", "Investigate: hair microscopy, immunoglobulins, lymphocyte subsets, zinc/biotin, skin biopsy; manage dehydration, hypernatraemia, temperature, sepsis"]),
 ("Why should systemic steroids be avoided in psoriatic erythroderma but may be used in others?", ["Steroid withdrawal precipitates generalised pustular psoriasis/ rebound erythroderma; in drug-induced (DRESS), eczematous/airborne contact, pemphigus foliaceus and some idiopathic cases steroids are useful with careful tapering"]),
 ("Fluid and nutrition care in erythroderma.", ["Daily weight, input–output charts; extra fluids for insensible losses (≈ 2–4 l/day extra), correct electrolytes (Na, K), monitor albumin; high-protein (1.5–2 g/kg) and high-calorie diet, vitamin/ iron/ folate supplements; avoid hypothermia (warm room 30–32 °C, blankets)"]),
 ("Cardiac complications and why.", ["Generalised cutaneous vasodilation increases blood flow (up to 10–20% of cardiac output) → high-output cardiac failure, tachycardia, especially in elderly with heart disease; monitor ECG, avoid fluid overload"]),
 ("Lymphadenopathy in erythroderma — dermatopathic vs lymphoma.", ["Dermatopathic lymphadenitis: reactive, soft, mobile, paracortical expansion with melanin- and lipid-laden macrophages and Langerhans cells", "Lymphoma: hard, rapidly enlarging, matted; atypical cells, clonal TCR — biopsy suspicious nodes (excisional)"]),
 ("Nail and hair changes in erythroderma.", ["Nails: Beau's lines, onycholysis, shiny polished nails (rubbing), subungual hyperkeratosis (psoriasis/PRP/crusted scabies), onychomadesis", "Hair: telogen effluvium, diffuse alopecia (CTCL may have alopecia with 'leonine facies'), madarosis"]),
 ("Erythroderma in HIV.", ["Causes: psoriasis, seborrhoeic dermatitis, drug reactions (nevirapine, cotrimoxazole), crusted scabies, PRP type VI, CTCL, chronic actinic dermatitis; check CD4 and viral load; treat cause and optimise ART"]),
 ("What is pityriasis rubra pilaris erythroderma's characteristic picture?", ["Orange-red erythroderma with sharply demarcated islands of sparing ('nappes claires'), follicular papules on knuckles, waxy orange keratoderma (sandal), fine bran-like scales; responds to retinoids/ methotrexate/ IL-17 inhibitors"]),
 ("Pemphigus foliaceus erythroderma — clues.", ["Crusted, scaly, oozing erosions with 'cornflake' scales on seborrhoeic areas, positive Nikolsky, no mucosal involvement, smell; biopsy (subcorneal acantholysis), DIF intercellular IgG, anti-Dsg1 ELISA"]),
 ("Crusted scabies presenting as erythroderma — clues.", ["Thick crusts over hands, feet, extensors, scalp and nails, sometimes little itch, immunocompromised/elderly/ institutionalised, outbreak among staff and contacts; mineral oil scraping shows abundant mites — isolate and treat with ivermectin + topical scabicide"]),
 ("What follow-up is needed after recovery?", ["Treat underlying disease, review drugs (drug card), monitor for relapse, repeat biopsies in idiopathic cases for CTCL every 6–12 months, maintain skin care with emollients"]),
 ("Describe the Sézary cell.", ["Atypical T lymphocyte with hyperchromatic convoluted ('cerebriform') nucleus, scant cytoplasm (CD4+, CD7−, CD26− typically); >1000/µl in blood (B2 blood involvement) defines Sézary syndrome with clonal TCR"]),
 ("Prognosis by cause in erythroderma.", ["Best: drug-induced (resolves in weeks after withdrawal), eczema", "Intermediate: psoriasis, PRP (relapses)", "Worst: Sézary syndrome/CTCL (median survival ~2–5 years), idiopathic in elderly (chronic, may evolve)"]),
 ("Exfoliative dermatitis in children due to SSSS — management.", ["Admit, IV anti-staphylococcal antibiotics (cloxacillin/ cefazolin; clindamycin to reduce toxin), fluids, temperature control, gentle skin care with emollients, analgesia; no steroids; heals without scarring in 1–2 weeks"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Lichen planus", qa=[
 ("Describe dermoscopy of LP.", ["Wickham's striae — white reticular/ radial streaks, 'white network' or 'leaf venation'; red dotted/ linear vessels at periphery; grey-blue dots (pigment incontinence) in resolving/pigmented lesions; useful to differentiate LP from psoriasis and lichenoid drug eruption"]),
 ("What is hepatitis C association and its relevance in India?", ["Associations vary geographically — strong in Japan and Mediterranean, weak in India; still advisable to test HCV/HBV in erosive oral LP and extensive disease; LP may flare with interferon therapy (older HCV treatment)"]),
 ("Describe genital LP and vulvovaginal-gingival syndrome.", ["Men: annular violaceous papules on glans, 'white lacy' lesions; women: erosive vulvar/vaginal LP with scarring, synechiae, introital stenosis, dyspareunia, + desquamative gingivitis (vulvovaginal-gingival syndrome) — chronic, risk of SCC; treat with clobetasol, tacrolimus, vaginal dilators"]),
 ("What is lichen planus pemphigoides vs bullous LP?", ["Bullous LP: blisters within LP lesions due to intense interface damage (Max-Joseph spaces) — DIF negative", "LP pemphigoides: LP plus tense blisters on previously normal skin with antibodies to BP180 (NC16A — MCW-4 epitope); DIF linear IgG/C3 at BMZ; treat like BP + LP (steroids, dapsone, tetracyclines)"]),
 ("Describe hypertrophic LP and its risks.", ["Thick hyperkeratotic verrucous plaques on shins/ankles, intensely itchy, chronic (years), pigmented; venous stasis association; may mimic or transform into SCC/keratoacanthoma (biopsy non-healing nodules)", "Treatment: intralesional/ occluded super-potent steroids, acitretin, PUVA, cryotherapy, methotrexate"]),
 ("Actinic lichen planus — features.", ["Children and young adults in sunny climates (India, Middle East); annular hyperpigmented plaques with hypopigmented halo or melasma-like patches on face, neck, dorsa of hands; little itch; worse in summer; photoprotection, HCQ, topical steroids, acitretin"]),
 ("LP of palms and soles — features.", ["Firm yellowish hyperkeratotic papules/ plaques, sometimes ulcerative LP of soles (painful ulcers, toenail loss — may need grafting); can mimic warts, calluses, psoriasis"]),
 ("Drug-induced oral lichenoid reactions vs oral LP.", ["Oral lichenoid lesions near amalgam fillings (contact — patch test to mercury; remove amalgam), unilateral, related to drugs (antimalarials, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, antihypertensives), GVHD; oral LP is bilateral, symmetrical"]),
 ("What is the Koebner phenomenon in LP?", ["Linear LP papules appear along scratch marks or trauma in ~20% of active disease (isomorphic response) — indicates active disease"]),
 ("Childhood LP — differences.", ["Less common; more often linear, eruptive/generalised, actinic and hypertrophic types; oral involvement less; LP-like drug eruption and lichen nitidus overlap; treat with topical steroids, NB-UVB, oral minipulse in extensive disease"]),
 ("Lichen nitidus — differentiate from LP.", ["Lichen nitidus: tiny (1–2 mm) shiny skin-coloured flat-topped papules in groups on penis, abdomen, forearms, children; Koebner; asymptomatic; histology — 'ball-and-claw' (well-circumscribed granulomatous infiltrate in a dermal papilla clasped by rete ridges); usually resolves spontaneously"]),
 ("What is LP–lupus overlap?", ["Patients have features of both LP and LE (atrophic violaceous plaques on extremities, scarring); histology and DIF show mixed features; ANA may be positive; treat with antimalarials and topical steroids"]),
 ("Systemic therapy of severe LP — doses.", ["Prednisolone 0.5–1 mg/kg/day for 4–6 weeks tapering, or betamethasone/dexamethasone OMP 5 mg on 2 consecutive days/week for 3–6 months", "Acitretin 25–35 mg/day for 8 weeks (strongest evidence among retinoids)", "Methotrexate 7.5–15 mg/week, metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 3–4 weeks (Indian studies), sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine (oral LP), azathioprine, MMF, cyclosporine; NB-UVB/PUVA 2–3 times weekly; JAK inhibitors for refractory"]),
 ("Prognosis for scarring forms.", ["LPP, FFA and nail LP (dorsal pterygium) cause permanent scarring/ hair or nail loss — need early aggressive treatment; erosive oral and vulvar LP are chronic with malignancy risk"]),
 ("What is the differential diagnosis of violaceous papules?", ["Lichen planus, lichenoid drug eruption, lichen nitidus, lichen striatus, guttate psoriasis, papular secondary syphilis, pityriasis rosea (papular), prurigo, LP-like keratosis (solitary), Kaposi sarcoma (HIV), GVHD (lichenoid)"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Atopic dermatitis", qa=[
 ("What is the role of S. aureus and how do you reduce it?", ["Colonises 70–90% of lesions (vs 5% healthy skin); superantigens (SEA, SEB, TSST-1) activate T cells and IgE production; δ-toxin degranulates mast cells; biofilms block sweat ducts", "Reduce: treat inflammation (steroids/ TCIs reduce colonisation), dilute bleach baths (0.005%) twice weekly, antiseptic washes (chlorhexidine), short courses of systemic antibiotics for clinically infected eczema; avoid long-term topical antibiotics (resistance)"]),
 ("What are wet wrap therapy and its indications?", ["After bath and emollient (± diluted topical steroid), apply a wet tubular bandage layer then a dry layer, for several hours/ overnight for 3–7 days — cools, reduces itch, enhances absorption; for severe flares/ children; watch for infection and steroid absorption"]),
 ("Describe topical calcineurin inhibitors in detail.", ["Tacrolimus ointment 0.03% (≥2 y) and 0.1% (adults); pimecrolimus 1% cream (≥3 months in some countries) — inhibit calcineurin → block T-cell IL-2 transcription", "No atrophy — ideal for face, eyelids, flexures, genitals; proactive twice weekly use reduces flares", "Side effects: burning/ stinging (first week), alcohol flushing, local infections; 'black box' lymphoma warning not substantiated by long-term data"]),
 ("Oral JAK inhibitors in AD — details.", ["Upadacitinib (15–30 mg/day, JAK1), abrocitinib (100–200 mg/day, JAK1), baricitinib (2–4 mg/day, JAK1/2) — rapid itch relief within days, high EASI-75 rates", "Screen: TB, hepatitis B/C, HIV, CBC, lipids, LFT, CK, pregnancy; risks — infections (herpes zoster), acne, raised CPK and lipids, cytopenias, VTE, MACE and malignancy (boxed warning in older smokers)"]),
 ("Systemic cyclosporine in AD — practical points.", ["3–5 mg/kg/day in two doses for short-term (3–6 months, up to 1–2 years) — rapid response; monitor BP and creatinine (reduce dose if >25–30% rise), potassium, magnesium, lipids; drug interactions (CYP3A4); taper to avoid rebound; suitable for children with severe disease"]),
 ("Methotrexate and azathioprine in AD.", ["Methotrexate 7.5–25 mg/week (0.3–0.5 mg/kg/week in children) with folic acid — slower onset (8–12 weeks), good long-term option, cheap", "Azathioprine 1–3 mg/kg/day, dose by TPMT activity (and NUDT15 in Asians); side effects — marrow suppression, hepatotoxicity, nausea, infection, skin cancer risk with UV"]),
 ("Phototherapy for AD.", ["NB-UVB 2–3 times weekly for chronic moderate disease (adults, older children); UVA1 (medium dose) for acute flares; PUVA rarely; avoid in photosensitive AD/ XP; maintain emollients"]),
 ("Explain 'proactive therapy'.", ["After clearing a flare, continue twice-weekly topical steroid (mid-potency) or tacrolimus on previously affected sites plus daily emollients — significantly reduces flare frequency compared with reactive use"]),
 ("Adult-onset AD — what to consider?", ["Exclude contact dermatitis (patch test), scabies, drug eruption, CTCL (biopsy), HIV, dermatitis herpetiformis; head-and-neck dermatitis (Malassezia-driven — antifungals), hand eczema (occupational); more often IgE-normal (intrinsic)"]),
 ("Intrinsic vs extrinsic AD.", ["Extrinsic (~80%): high total IgE, specific IgE to aeroallergens/foods, personal/family atopy, FLG mutations more", "Intrinsic (~20%): normal IgE, no specific sensitisation, female, later onset, milder, nickel/metal allergy more common"]),
 ("Ocular complications of AD and dupilumab.", ["Atopic keratoconjunctivitis, vernal keratoconjunctivitis, keratoconus (eye rubbing), anterior/posterior subcapsular cataract (disease + steroids), retinal detachment; dupilumab-associated conjunctivitis (goblet-cell loss; treat with lubricants, tacrolimus eye ointment, steroid drops) — ophthalmology referral"]),
 ("Psychosocial burden and sleep in AD.", ["Itch–scratch-related sleep loss (child and parents), ADHD association, depression/anxiety, school/work absenteeism, family stress; measure with POEM, CDLQI/DLQI, sleep scores; sedating antihistamines short-term at night, behavioural therapy, education programmes ('eczema school')"]),
 ("What is the role of probiotics, vitamin D and dietary supplements?", ["Evidence is weak/inconsistent: probiotics in pregnancy/infancy may modestly reduce AD risk; vitamin D supplementation may help winter-related flares in deficient children; evening primrose oil, fish oil — not recommended"]),
 ("What is follicular (papular) eczema?", ["Pattern common in darker skin (African, Indian): tiny skin-coloured follicular papules on trunk/extensors giving 'goosebump' appearance; part of AD; responds to emollients and mild steroids"]),
 ("Prevention of AD in high-risk infants — current evidence.", ["Daily emollients from birth — large trials (BEEP, PreventADALL) showed no prevention; exclusive breastfeeding for 4–6 months is encouraged for other benefits; early introduction of allergenic foods (peanut/egg from 4–6 months) reduces food allergy"]),
 ("Differentiate AD from scabies in an infant.", ["Scabies: burrows, palms/soles and axillary nodules, other family members itching, acropustules, rapid onset", "AD: chronic relapsing, cheeks/extensors, family history of atopy, xerosis; both can coexist — scrape suspicious lesions"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Contact dermatitis", qa=[
 ("What is the Indian standard series and how is it different from the European?", ["Indian Standard Series (Contact and Occupational Dermatoses Forum of India) — ~20–25 allergens chosen for local exposures: parthenium, PPD, nickel, chromate, cobalt, fragrance mix, colophony, neomycin, nitrofurazone, gentian violet, mercaptobenzothiazole, thiuram, black rubber mix, epoxy resin, formaldehyde, parabens, benzocaine, etc.", "European Baseline Series (~30) includes newer preservatives (methylisothiazolinone), budesonide/ tixocortol, textile dye mix, propolis, sesquiterpene lactone mix; supplementary Indian series exist (cosmetic, vegetable, footwear, hair-dye)"]),
 ("What is the repeated open application test (ROAT)?", ["Patient applies the suspected product (cosmetic/ medicament) twice daily to the antecubital fossa for 7–14 days; a reaction confirms clinical relevance of a weak or doubtful patch test; useful for leave-on cosmetics"]),
 ("What is the 'use test' and 'semi-open test'?", ["Use test: applying the product as normally used to a limited area", "Semi-open test: for irritant products (shampoos, paints) — small amount applied, allowed to dry, then covered, to avoid irritant reactions"]),
 ("Pathogenesis of irritant contact dermatitis.", ["Direct damage to stratum corneum lipids and keratinocytes (surfactants, solvents, alkalis) → barrier disruption, TEWL, release of IL-1α, TNF-α, IL-8 from keratinocytes (innate inflammation, no memory); factors — concentration, duration, occlusion, frequency, atopy (barrier), age, site (thin skin), climate"]),
 ("Hand dermatitis in health workers — approach.", ["Wet work and frequent handwashing (ICD), rubber glove allergy (thiurams, carbamates), latex contact urticaria (type I — anaphylaxis risk), chlorhexidine/ fragrance; prefer alcohol hand rubs, cotton-lined synthetic (nitrile) gloves, emollients after washing; patch and prick test"]),
 ("Rubber allergy — types and alternatives.", ["Type IV to rubber accelerators — thiuram (gloves), carbamates, mercaptobenzothiazole (footwear), black rubber mix (PPD derivatives — tyres, rubber handles) — use vinyl/ nitrile gloves labelled accelerator-free", "Type I latex allergy — natural rubber latex proteins (Hev b); cross-reacts with banana, avocado, kiwi, chestnut (latex–fruit syndrome); use non-latex gloves"]),
 ("Footwear dermatitis — features.", ["Symmetrical dermatitis on dorsa of feet and toes sparing web spaces (vs tinea), may involve soles in rubber sole allergy; allergens — rubber accelerators (MBT), chromate (leather), adhesives (p-tertiary butylphenol formaldehyde resin — PTBP, also causes leucoderma), dyes; change to vegetable-tanned/ PVC/ cloth footwear"]),
 ("Cosmetic allergy — common culprits.", ["Fragrances (fragrance mix I/II, balsam of Peru, cinnamal, isoeugenol), preservatives (methylisothiazolinone — 'epidemic' in Europe, formaldehyde releasers — quaternium-15, DMDM hydantoin; parabens rarely), PPD in hair dyes, kumkum (Sudan I, brilliant lake red R), sunscreen filters, lanolin, propolis, nail acrylates (methacrylates — fingertip dermatitis, gel nails)"]),
 ("Medicament allergy — what to suspect?", ["Worsening of a dermatitis despite treatment, or leg ulcer/ stasis dermatitis/ otitis externa/ perianal dermatitis spreading — neomycin, framycetin, gentamicin, benzocaine, lignocaine, nitrofurazone, povidone-iodine, chlorhexidine, steroids (budesonide/tixocortol — groups A, B, D), lanolin, emulsifiers"]),
 ("What is pigmented contact dermatitis (Riehl's melanosis)?", ["Minimal eczema with grey-brown facial pigmentation in darker skin due to repeated low-grade allergy to fragrances, cosmetic dyes (brilliant lake red), kumkum, hair dyes; histology — interface damage and pigment incontinence; patch test to identify allergen; strict avoidance, photoprotection"]),
 ("Discuss airborne contact dermatitis causes besides parthenium.", ["Other Compositae (chrysanthemum, sunflower, Xanthium), Lantana, Ageratum, wood dust, pollen, epoxy resin vapours, fragrance sprays, insecticides, cement/ fibreglass dust (irritant), Primula, Toxicodendron smoke; clue — involvement of eyelids, behind ears, submental, V-neck, flexures"]),
 ("What are the newer treatments for chronic hand eczema?", ["Delgocitinib 2% cream (topical pan-JAK inhibitor, approved for moderate–severe chronic hand eczema), alitretinoin 30 mg/day (chronic hyperkeratotic), dupilumab (atopic hand eczema), oral JAK inhibitors under study; PUVA soaks"]),
 ("Prognosis of occupational contact dermatitis.", ["Poor — only 25–30% clear completely; many continue despite job change (persistent post-occupational dermatitis); better with early diagnosis, education, allergen avoidance; chromate allergy has worst prognosis"]),
 ]),
]
